# Untitled

Recent Progress of Metal Organic Frameworks-Based Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution, Oxygen Evolution, and Oxygen Reduction Reaction
Yaling Jia, Ziqian Xue, Yinle Li, and Guangqin Li\(^{\text{D}}\)
Exploring efficient and cost-saving electrocatalysts is essential to the renewable energy storage and utilization, which is still in its embryonic period. MOFs have drawn tremendous attention due to their adjustability, abundant active sites, and plentiful pores. Notably, satisfactory electrocatalytic performance has been achieved by MOFs-based electrocatalysts comparable to traditional electrocatalysts. State-of-the-art works about the MOFs-based electrocatalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), oxygen evolution reaction (OER), and ORR were summarized in this review. This review comprises a series of modifying strategies of MOFs and their derivatives, from aspects of structure, composition, and morphology. Furthermore, the active sites and functional mechanisms' recognition are involved in this review expecting to provide reference for rationally designing efficient electrocatalysts. At last, the current status, challenges, and perspectives of MOFs-based electrocatalysts are also discussed.
## 1. Introduction
The increasing consumption of fossil fuels leads to energy shortage and environmental crisis like global warming, which requires researchers to focus on more efficient energy storage and conversion systems.[1–4] Thus, fuel cells and water splitting techniques now thrive rapidly,[5,6] while their core reactions, such as HER, OER, and ORR, are kinetically sluggish. Therefore, many efforts have been devoted to explore efficient electrocatalysts. It is well known that \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) functioned well for OER and Pt shows incredible activity for HER and ORR.[7–9] Nonetheless, the high cost and scarcity of these precious metal-containing materials hinder their production at a larger scale.[10,11] A significant advancement in developing nonprecious metal electrocatalysts to replace the noble metal catalysts has been achieved lately.[12–14] And a substitutive electrocatalyst is supposed to meet these requirements: excellent catalytic activity, good robustness, low cost, and good conductivity.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as a new class of porous organic–inorganic hybrid materials were formed by the coordination of organic linkers and inorganic metal nodes.[15] The particular advantages of MOFs, such as high specific surface area, low density, large accessible pore volumes, and adjustable chemical components, confer MOFs with various applications, for example, gas sorption/separation and conversion,[16–18] catalysis,[19] sensors,[20] proton/ion conduction,[21] and drug carrying/delivery.[22] In particular, their adjustable structure and sufficient active sites present potential in the field of electrocatalysis.[23] For original MOFs, researchers obtained admirable electrocatalytic performance by structure (electronic structure, lattice strain, etc.) and morphology regulation.[24–27] Converting MOFs into derivatives is another rational strategy to improve performance while retaining their merits.[28] This review mainly summarizes the recent progress of MOF-based materials as electrocatalysts for HER, OER, and ORR from aspects of strategies, active sites' identification, the remaining challenges, and the future prospect.
