## 1.1.2. Activity Evaluation for HER, OER, and ORR
In HER and OER, the overpotential at a fixed current density (10/50/ \(100\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\)) (η) is generally recognized as a vital evaluation parameter for electrocatalyst activity, which to a certain degree represents the intrinsic performance of an electrocatalyst.[35,43] For ORR, the onset potential, half-wave potential, kinetic current density at a specific potential, and mass activity are commonly used to evaluate the electrocatalytic performance of a electrocatalyst.
With continuous in-depth development of nanomaterial electrocatalysts, it is unfair to directly regard the geometrical area of the flat electrode as electrochemical active area due to the large surface area of nano-electrocatalysts.[44] Thus, the electrochemical surface area is measured by cycling the electrodes with different scan rates at non-Faradic regions. The slope of the linear relationship between scan rates and non-Faraday current density designates the electrical double-layer capacitance \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)\). Then the roughness factor is obtained by dividing the double-layer capacitance of a flat surface electrode from normalizing the double-layer capacitance \(\left(\mathrm{ECSA}=\frac{\mathrm{C_{dl}}}{\mathrm{ECSA}}\right)\). To compare the performance results from different nano-electrocatalysts, the normalized current density \(\left(\mathrm{j_{ECSA}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{ECSA}}\right)\) is strongly encouraged in the literature.[45]
Tafel slope. Tafel slope is a primary indicator to evaluate the performance of an electrocatalyst. A small Tafel slope value indicates a high electron transfer ability and rapid kinetics of the selected catalyst.[46–48] For HER and OER, the linear slope of the plot of \(\log(\mathrm{j})\) versus \(\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}\) is defined as the Tafel slope, while for ORR the measured current density should be substituted by the kinetic current density according to \(J_{\mathrm{K}}^{-1}=J^{-1}-J_{\mathrm{L}}^{-1}\), where J designates the measured current density at a specific potential, \(J_{\mathrm{K}}\) refers to the kinetic current density, and \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) is the limited diffusion current density.[49]
Furthermore, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was carried out to investigate the charge-transfer kinetics at the electrode and electrolyte interface. The semicircular diameter of Nyquist plot designates the electrochemical impedance, and a smaller value denotes a smaller charge-transfer resistance.[50]
## 1.2. Brief Introduction of MOFs
Synthesis methods of MOFs mainly include solvothermal process, microwave heating, ultrasound treatment, slow diffusion at dual
phase interface, seed crystal growth, and so on, which determine topologies and structures of the target MOFs.[51] With many specific advantages, such as structural tunability, high specific surface area, and abundant active sites, MOFs appeared in the field of electrocatalysis. Pre-synthetic design or post-synthetic modification on basic building units of MOFs, including metal nodes/clusters/linkers or coordination bonds, could reap various topologies or/and structures with expanded functionality. For example, adjusting their electronic structure to optimize the intermediate adsorption properties is an efficient strategy to enhance electrocatalytic performance of MOFs.[24,52,53] In addition to pristine MOFs as efficient electrocatalysts,[54] MOFs were also combined with other conductive materials to form MOFs composites or were employed as self-sacrificed precursors/templates to produce derivatives inheriting the merits of the original MOFs.[50,55,56] Derivation of MOFs usually accessed porous carbon materials loaded with nanoparticles, such as metal nanoparticles, metal alloys, metal oxides, chalcogenides, phosphides, nitrides, and carbides.[19,41,57] These MOFs-derived materials have exhibited an excellent electrocatalytic performance.[58]
In the electrocatalytic process, the abundant metal sites of MOFs become the potential active sites on which occur the adsorption and desorption of the reaction intermediate. The large surface area and a wealth of pores are in favor of mass transfer. Its morphology structure engineering conduces to exposing more active sites and increase active sites' utilization. MOFs as precursors possess unique merits compared with traditional methods. These agree with the requirements of an efficient electrocatalyst for HER, OER, and ORR.
Although there are some previous detailed reviews on MOFs-related materials as electrocatalysts, this review presents meritorious examples in the recent progress of MOFs-based materials for HER, OER, and ORR. The recognition of active sites and reaction mechanisms are also involved in this review, hoping to provide direction for rationally designing efficient electrocatalysts. In the end, the current development status, challenges, and perspectives are proposed.
## 2. Pristine MOFs and MOFs Composites as Electrocatalysts
Previously, many pristine MOFs or MOFs composites were directly applied as electrocatalysts with a good electrocatalytic property benefiting from large specific surface area, abundant micropores, and numerous unsaturated metal ions' active sites of MOFs. Their isolated active sites play a vital role in electrocatalysis, and their large surface area and high porosity could increase the contact area between the reactant and catalyst, thus promoting mass transfer. Nevertheless, unsatisfying intrinsic activity and low conductivity of pristine MOFs hindered their further expanded access to electrocatalytic applications. Thus, tremendous attempts have been devoted to increase the intrinsic activity of metal sites and the number of accessible active sites via manipulating their composition and morphology.[59] Their tunable structure, varied morphology, and regulable crystal structure are the adjusting instruments to regulate the electrocatalytic performance of MOFs. To be specific, there have been many proposed resultful methods, including metal node engineering, linker engineering, morphology engineering, lattice-strained MOFs,[24] etc., to ameliorate the performance of MOFs. Besides, growing pristine MOFs on highly conductive soft or hard substrates such as carbon materials, and Ni or Cu foam, is an alternative efficient way to overcome the conductivity issue of MOFs.[2] In
summary, up to now, three strategies have been adopted to improve the electrocatalytic efficiency of MOFs: 1) modulating the coordination environment of MOF by modifying its linker, metal center, lattice structure;[60,61] 2) regulating the growth condition of MOFs to obtain ultrathin MOFs nanosheet or nanobarray which benefits rapid mass transport, electron transfer, and increased catalytic active sites;[23,62] and 3) coupling MOFs with functionally materials or any other active constituent.[63] On the whole, the fundamental principles of design strategies mainly focused on increasing exposed active sites and regulating the electronic structure of MOFs to optimize the intermediate adsorption/desorption properties.
## 2.1. Pristine MOFs and MOFs Composites for HER
