## 2.2. Pristine MOFs and MOFs Composites for OER
So far, many attempts have been made to enhance the OER activity of MOFs via modifying them from aspects of composition, structure, and morphology (Table 2). Based on the single metal MOFs, additional metals were introduced in the process of preparation to modify their metal active center, thus regulating the OER performance of original MOFs.[68,69]Instead of directly mixing metal sources during MOFs preparation, Zhang group first employed a modular and stepwise method to synthesize a hybrid MOF which immobilized the labile dicobalt clusters \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{RCOO})_{4}(\mathrm{LT})_{2}\) \(\mathrm{R}=\) substituent group, \(\mathrm{LT}=\) terminal ligand) in a MOF (Figure 3). The synthesized MOF coated on Ni foam exhibited an exceptionally high electrocatalytic performance toward OER at \(\mathrm{pH}=13\) with an overpotential of \(225~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 3b,c).[70]
Notwithstanding abundant applied bimetallic MOFs exhibited a superior electrocatalytic performance, the underlying mechanism remains elusive. More recently, Yaqian Lan group carried out DFT calculations and experimental comparison toward a series of isostructural \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}^{2}\) -BPTC MOFs: \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\) -BPTC (NNU-21), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Co}\) -BPTC (NNU-22), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\) -BPTC (NNU-23), and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Zn}\) -BPTC (NNU-24). Among these MOFs, NNU-23 exhibits a superior OER catalytic performance than most previously reported MOF-based OER electrocatalysts. DFT calculation demonstrates that the introduction of the second metal could make the d-band center closer to the Fermi level and form a stronger binding interaction between the adsorbates and catalysts, thus enhancing the OER catalytic performance.[71]
In addition to metal site modification, Li group rationally modulated the electronic structure of MOFs by ligand modification. Specifically, two organic linkers (terephthalic acid which was denoted by "A" and 2-aminoterephthalic acid which was denoted by "B") and two metal ion centers (Fe and Co) were employed to synthesize multivariate MOFs \(\mathrm{A_{m}B-M O F-F e C o_{1.6}}\) using the solvothermal method (Figure 4). The optimized ratio of \(\mathrm{A}_{2.7}\mathrm{B}\) -MOF- \(\mathrm{\cdotFeCo_{1.6}}\) exhibits the best OER activity with an overpotential of \(288~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) and a low Tafel slope of \(39\mathrm{\mV\dec}^{-1}\) .[60] Based on the former observation, Li group continued to explore the modulating mechanism of the imported ligand on the electronic structure of MOFs. In their work, various missing linkers were incorporated into a layered-pillared MOF \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right)\) (CoBDC) and the self-supported Co-BDC nanoarray with missing linker of carboxyferrocene exhibited excellent OER performance with ultralow overpotential of \(241~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 5a,b).[61] The authors observed the signal of cobalt oxyhydroxide (CoOOH) from X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectrum, implying the part of MOF turns into CoOOH after OER stability.
Except for the electronic structure modification strategy, shaping the morphology of MOFs as 2D nanosheet or nanoarray also exhibits an apparent performance improvement. A bimetallic NiFe-based MOF nanosheet supported on Ni foam directly acted as electrode for electrochemical OER and exhibited excellent catalytic activity and robustness.[72,73] By converting ultrasonic thin MOFs into 2D ultrathin nanosheets, Tang et al. prepared ultrathin NiCo bimetallic MOF
nanosheets (NiCo-UMOFNs), which demonstrated an overpotential of \(250~\mathrm{mV}\) at a current density of \(10\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) in alkaline conditions for OER. And when NiCo-UMOFNs were loaded on copper foam, the overpotential could be decreased to \(189\,\mathrm{\mV}\) to achieve the same current density.[68] The electrocatalytic performance was affected by the morphology and structure. A general approach to grow 2D MOF ultrathin nanoarray through dissolution–crystallization mechanism was reported. This MOF is composed of alternating
organic linker (2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylic group) and inorganic metal-oxygen layers \(\left(\mathrm{MO}_{6}\right.\) units; \(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Ni}\), Fe, or \(\mathrm{Cu}\)) (Figure 6a). Compared with the bulk powder MOFs, their corresponding ultrathin MOF nanoarrays exhibit a significantly enhanced electrocatalytic activity for OER, HER, and overall water splitting, benefited from highly exposed active metal sites, improved electrical conductivity, and presented a combination of hierarchical porous architectures (Figure 6b,c).[23] Li group employed ligand-assisted strategy to
synthesize 2D CoFe-BDC from the transformation of 2D LDH (Figure 7). This unique 2D characteristic and synergistic effect contributes to the superior water oxidation reaction performance of the formed electrocatalyst 2D CoFe-BDC.[74]
Current reports demonstrate that incorporating MOFs with other active constituents, such as another MOF nanoparticle, oxide, and molecular complex, is also an efficient strategy to enhance the OER performance of MOFs. For example, Sun group synthesized an active 2D MOF nanosheet decorated with Fe-MOF nanoparticles. The MOFs hybrid exhibited \(265~\mathrm{mV}\) of overpotential at \(10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) , ascribed to the synergistic effect between Ni and Fe active centers.[75] Metal oxide was combined with MOF to enhance the electrocatalytic OER performance. Ultrafine \(\mathrm{CoFeO_{x}}\) nanoparticles were formed in the lattice of a Co-MOF, which was then further exfoliated into a monolayered heterogeneous nanosheet involving the inorganic nanoparticles. The valence of Co sites was increased and 3d electronic configurations were
changed at the interface between metal oxide nanoparticles and monolayer MOF, which leads to an enhanced electrocatalytic OER performance.[76] Ding group immobilized covalent Ru molecular complexes on MOFs MIL-101, which leads to the enhancement of efficiency and robustness of molecular catalysts.[77]
Integrating with conductive supports could enhance the charge-transfer efficiency of MOFs. Promising conductive supports including carbon material (graphene oxide (GO)),[78] and Maxene were hybridized with a 2D MOF Co-BDC via an interdiffusion reaction-assisted process. Benefiting from the highly porous structure of the Co-BDC layer and the electrically conductive \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) nanosheets which enabled the rapid charge and ion transfer across the well-defined \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}-\mathrm{Co}-\) BDC interface, thus facilitating the access of aqueous electrolyte to the catalytically active Co-BDC surfaces, the resulting hybrid material achieved an overpotential of \(410~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) with a Tafel slope of \(48.2\ \mathrm{mV\dec}^{-1}\) in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}\) .[79]
## 2.3. Pristine MOFs and MOFs Composites for ORR
Although MOFs have attracted tremendous interest directly as electrocatalysts owing to their high surface area, abundant hierarchical pores, and the high density of active sites, their poor conductivity hinders the enhancement of their electrocatalytic performance. Under this circumstance, conductive MOFs have drawn tremendous efforts and exhibit
satisfactory electrocatalytic efficiency for ORR (Table 3). This type of MOF usually consists of \(\pi\) -conjugated ligands, such as benzenehexathiol,[80] 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP),[81] and hexaiminobenzene,[82] and metal ions. Porphyrin and phthalocyaninebased MOFs as a kind of 2D conjugated MOFs have drawn much attention due to their better electron transfer and more exposed active sites.[83] MOFs of metal-catecholates (M-CATs) with specific \(\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) active sites were constructed at an atomic-level crystal structure and exhibited superior electrocatalytic activity for ORR.[84] A conductive 2D layered material \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{HITP}=2,3,6,7,10,11\) - hexaiminotriphenylene) with satisfying electrical conductivity \((\sigma=40\ S\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) was introduced and exhibited ORR activity.[85] Furthermore, Peng group investigated the role of metal centers in modulating the activity of ORR by varying the metal ratio \(\mathrm{(Ni/Co}\) ratio) in coordination polymers with HITP as the ligand. Experimental results revealed that unpaired 3d electrons of metal Co in \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}\) promote ORR activity.[86] Dong group investigated a variety of \(\pi\) -conjugated metal bis(dithiolene) complex nanosheets \(\mathrm{MC}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) (Fe, Co, Ni, Ru, Rh, Pd, Os, Ir, and Pt) for the ORR. It is found that the central atoms determine the adsorption energies of ORR intermediates, thus affecting the ORR activity, and the \(\mathrm{IrC}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanosheets exhibit optimal performance among the studied varied \(\mathrm{MC}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) .[87] A duallinked hexaiminobenzene metal organic framework (Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF)-based bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst was demonstrated, with half-wave potential of 0.883 V.[82] Recently, Chen group regulated the substitute group of ligand from coordination polymer to enhance its activity. The reason is that the substitute groups could lift the \(\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{xz}}/\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{yz}}\) orbitals of transition metal centers, which enhances intermediate adsorption (Figure 7).[88] The MOFs, which consist of ligand possessing large conjugate system including porphyrin and phthalocyanine group, also present a noticeable performance for ORR.[83]
Combining MOFs with highly conductive materials is an operative approach to enhance the conductivity of MOFs and improve the electrocatalytic activity of MOFs. PCN-223 constructed from \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) oxo clusters and Fe(III) porphyrin linkers was combined with conductive fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) to promote ORR.[89] NPC-4 MOF compound \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{TMBDI})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{TMBDI}=2,3,5,6\) -tetramethylbenzene-1,4-di-isophthalate) whose nanocages were activated by the solvent-exchange method combined with reduced graphene oxide (RGO) were modified onto a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) as an excellent nonprecious metal electrocatalyst for ORR. This research first used
tive fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) to promote ORR.[89] NPC-4 MOF compound \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{TMBDI})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{TMBDI}=2,3,5,6\) -tetramethylbenzene-1,4-di-isophthalate) whose nanocages were activated by the solvent-exchange method combined with reduced graphene oxide (RGO) were modified onto a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) as an excellent nonprecious metal electrocatalyst for ORR. This research first used
RGO as a binder and electron transfer mediator, offering a new route to develop MOFs as electrocatalyst.[90] Interestingly, N-doped pomelo-peel-derived carbon integrated with zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67) afforded a substantially enhanced ORR electrocatalytic performance comparable to that of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) in alkaline media attributed to the synergistic factors, including \(\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}\) active sites in ZIF-67, large surface area, and the strong interaction in this hybrid.[91]
Similarly, integrating MOFs with other active components is another operative approach to improve the electrocatalytic activity of MOFs. Cao group grafts Co-porphyrins on MOF surfaces (ZIF-8/ZIF-67) (zeolitic imidazolate framework) via ligand exchange strategy (Figure 8). The obtained Co-porphyrin@ZIF-8 showed a significant increase of activity compared with the ungrafted one.[92]
## 2.4. Recognition of the Active Species of Electrocatalysts
Both metal sites and nonmetal sites have ever been reported to be active sites for HER. Generally, the metal sites of MOFs and some compounds as active sites usually proceeds by the M-H adsorption during HER (Figure 9).[67,94,95] Meanwhile, the nonmetal atoms are also identified as active sites for nonmetal-doped materials in some literatures.[96]
Benefiting from the booming development of in situ techniques, the structural transformation of electrocatalysts was clearly recorded. Tang group selected MOF-74 as the model OER electrocatalyst and investigated its structural change by employing operando X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) images.[97] They discovered that NiCo-MOF-74 was first transformed to \(\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) which then transformed to oxygen-vacant oxyhydroxide \(\mathrm{V_{O}^{\bullet\bullet}\mathrm{-Ni_{0.5}C o_{0.5}O O H_{0.75}}}\) at a higher overpotential. \(\mathrm{V_{O}^{\bullet\bullet}\mathrm{-Ni_{0.5}C o_{0.5}O O H_{0.75}}}\) plays an active role during the OER process. Interestingly, when the voltage was removed and the electrocatalyst was then put in the air, the formed \(\mathrm{V_{O}^{\bullet\bullet}\mathrm{-Ni_{0.5}C o_{0.5}O O H_{0.75}}}\) was converted to \(\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) then to original MOF-74. While this transformation is not instantaneous, the complete conversion to original MOF consumes more than 10 days. On account
of its nonimmediate change, Li group could still observe CoOOH after the OER process.[61]
The sites \(\mathrm{M-N(O/S)_{4}}\) in plane are usually taken for active sites of ORR in 2D conjugation polymers.[98] Their sufficient active sites and conjugation plane structure attribute to their excellent performance. However, the active site evolution during electrocatalysis is still obscure.
Besides the above in situ characterization techniques, theory calculation is an important instruction to investigate the electronic structure of active sites. An ideal electrocatalyst should possess sufficient intermediate adsorption strength to facilitate the electron-transfer process, and it also should possess a weak enough binding strength with product molecule to promote the escape of product.[99] DFT calculations could provide
relationship between the inherent electronic structure (especially the d-orbital of metal) and intermediate adsorption strength, which would definitely provide direction to modify electrocatalyst from the aspect of electronic structure.
## 3. MOFs-Derived Materials as Electrocatalysts
MOFs-derived materials could not only inherit the merits from original MOFs, such as the morphology with high specific surface area, the large number of pores, and the diversity in metal center, but also make for their unfavorable conductibility, which confer the derived materials excellent electrocatalytic activity for HER, OER, and ORR.[100– 105] The high-temperature pyrolysis and room-temperature transformation are commonly employed strategies to obtain the target MOFs derivatives.[106] MOFs-derived materials generally consist of porous carbon, metal, or metal alloy encapsulated in carbon, metal compound (oxides, carbides, nitrides, chalcogenides, and phosphides), single metal atom, etc.[107,108] MOFs-derived transition
metal-based materials have drawn tremendous research efforts due to their three special merits: 1) MOFs-derived carbon as support not only promotes electron transfer and ions’ diffusion, but also isolates transition metal hybrid from aggregation during the electrocatalytic process; 2) Evenly distributed transition metal hybrid in porous carbon is conducive to exposing more accessible active sites; and 3) Various heteroatoms doping could tailor the electronic structure of metal atoms or neighboring carbon atoms.[58]
Single-atom catalysts as newly emerging catalyst species have aroused widespread attention due to their high atom utilization and special size effect, which afford preferable performance to metal nanoparticle.[109–112] However, their high surface free energy made them prone to migration and agglomeration.[113] Due to the highly ordered arrangement of metal nodes and organic ligands as well as the well-defined porosity of MOFs, MOF became a good platform for the fabrication of single-atom catalyst.[114,115] With the development of material characterization equipment, especially XAS and the combination of high-angle annular dark-field imaging (HAADF)-scanning transmission electron microscope (STEM) images and electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS), it is easy to investigate the surrounding environment of metal single atoms at the atomic level.[116] Nevertheless, the applications of single-atom catalysts are in their infancy and need further efforts to achieve industrialization.[119,117]
## 3.1. MOFs-Derived Electrocatalysts for HER
MOFs-derived materials, such as metal nanoparticles loaded on carbon material, phosphides, sulfides, carbides, and single-atom catalyst, were found to exhibit a favorable HER performance (Table 4).[118] For example, Chen group synthesized ultrafine Co nanoparticles
encapsulated in nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (CNTs) grafted onto both sides of RGO (Co@N-CNTs@rGO), utilizing bimetallic MOFs as precursors and (GO as template by direct annealing treatment. By virtue of the uniform distribution of Co nanoparticles, the highly graphitic NCNTs @rGO, the large surface area, and the abundant porosity, the as-prepared Co@N-CNTs@rGO exhibited quintessential electrocatalytic activity for HER both in alkaline and acidic media, with current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) at a lower overpotential of 108 and \(87\ \mathrm{mV}\) in \(1\ \mathrm{M}\) KOH and \(0.5\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\), respectively, superior to most of the co-based electrocatalysts for HER.[119]
Yan group synthesized a \(3\mathrm{D}\ \mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}\) polyhedron with a hierarchically porous structure by a in situ synthesis strategy, derived from a nickel-centered MOF (MOF-74-Ni) by direct low-temperature phosphorization. The developed \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}\) polyhedron exhibited a superior electrocatalytic performance toward the HER than \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}\) particles, with a low overpotential of \(158\ \mathrm{mV}\) at the cathodic current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and a small Tafel slope of \(73\ \mathrm{mV\ dec}^{-1}\).[120] Unlike the commonly employed MOF-conversion conditions (e.g., heat treatment), a facile room-temperature electrochemical method was proposed to convert ZIF-67 films into amorphous cobalt sulfides (a-CoSx), which was proven to be a highly active and stable electrocatalyst for HER.[121] A hierarchical porous nano-composite sheet of N-doped
graphene oxide (NGO) and nickel sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{Ni}_{7}\mathrm{S}_{6}\right)\) (NGO/ \(\mathrm{Ni}_{7}\mathrm{S}_{6}\)) was fabricated deriving from MOF-74 with thiourea as the sulfur source and exhibited bifunctional activity for both OER and HER in alkaline electrolyte.[122]
Carbides have received great emphasis among the non-noble metal electrocatalysts due to their precious metal-like electronic structure, high conductivity, and outstanding HER activity and stability in wide pH.[103,123–125] By modifying MOF precursors, the porous carbon supported carbides can be easily obtained by controllable calcination.[126–129] Benefiting from the evenly distributed carbides and porous carbon facilitating charge transfer, the MOFs-derived carbides
show promising electrocatalytic activity and stability for HER.[124] For instance, Lou rationally synthesized ultrafine dual-phase carbide confined in porous nitrogen-doped carbon dodecahedrons (PNCDs) as an efficient HER catalyst. PNCDs were prepared by substituting ZIF-8 with an appropriate number of \(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}/\mathrm{WO}_{4}\) units and then subjected to annealing (Figure 10). Coupling interactions between MC and \(\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{C}\) in the dual-phase \(\mathrm{MC-M}_{2}\mathrm{C/PNCDs}\) is in favor of moderate adsorption/ desorption capability of adsorbed hydrogen intermediates, which was also proved by the experimental result that dual-phase \(\mathrm{MC-M}_{2}\mathrm{C/PNCD}\) exhibits a much better performance than single-phase MC/PNCDs or \(\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{C/PNCD}\).
Recently, another novel metal atom tungsten-based electrocatalyst has been investigated. Chen et al. reported a single-W-atom HER catalyst supported on MOF-derived N-doped carbon, which exhibited high activity equal to that of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\). DFT calculation verified that the \(\mathrm{W}_{1}\mathrm{N}_{1}\mathrm{C}_{3}\) moiety plays an important part in enhancing the HER performance.[130]
## 3.2. MOFs-derived electrocatalysts for OER
Although a great deal of research has been devoted to modifying pristine MOFs from aspects of structure, morphology, and composition
toward OER, their electrocatalytic activity needs further improvement. Among the currently researched OER electrocatalysts, MOFs-derived metal or alloy-supported porous carbon has attracted substantial attention.[131–135] MOFs can be converted into metal or alloy nanoparticles encapsulated in nitrogen-doped carbon materials by facile thermal decomposition in an inert atmosphere, which are highly catalytically active for OER attributed to the catalytic sites of metal and protection effect from outer carbon layers.[133,134,136] Besides, MOFs-derived compound (sulfide, phosphide, oxide, etc.) prepared by heat treatment or room-temperature direct conversion also reveals great potential in this application (Table 5).
Different from the traditional pyrolysis strategy, Qiu group presented a novel method using microwave field to obtain carbon fiber (CF)-supported cobalt nanocatalysts (Co-NC/CF) with high yield (up to \(48.7\ \mathrm{wt\%}\)) quickly (in just \(60\ \mathrm{s}\)). The prepared Co-NC/CF exhibits an
outstanding OER performance, reaching \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) at an overpotential of \(246\ \mathrm{mV}\) with a Tafel slope of \(68\ \mathrm{mV\ dec}^{-1}\). The remarkable catalytic activity came from the vertically aligned Co-NC nanosheets with more open structure which could provide more accessible area, the decreased adsorption free energy affected by the coated N-doped carbon layer coated on metallic Co featured active site, and the high content of carbon preventing Co nanoparticles from aggregation.[137]
\(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) was first used as active species cooperated with MOFs-derived carbon nanorods as the OER catalyst by Li group. These OER catalyst 1D \(\mathrm{MoO}_{2}\mathrm{-Co}_{2}\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{8}\mathrm{C}\) nanorods were synthesized using
\(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\) composites as a precursor subjected to further heat treatment under inert atmosphere. The as-prepared OER catalyst presents superior OER catalytic activity in alkaline solution, with a low overpotential of \(320\ \mathrm{mV\ dec}^{-1}\), and a relatively good durability, which is attributed to the highly active \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{MoO}_{2}\) species and the unique structure of the catalyst.[138] Mosaic-structured cobalt–nickel phosphosphate nanosheets incorporating N-doped carbon were prepared by using Co–Ni Prussian-blue analog (PBA) nanoplates as templating precursors which suffered from simultaneous sulfuration, phosphorization, and the final sonication. The synthesized cobalt–nickel thiophosphate nanosheets were demonstrated to be an efficient OER electrocatalyst in \(1.0\ \mathrm{M\ KOH}\) with low overpotentials of \(262\ \mathrm{mV}\) at \(30\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), due to the higher intrinsic activity, better conductivity, and lower kinetic energy barrier.[139] A Co-doped nickel selenide \(\left(\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{Se}_{4}\right)/\mathrm{C}\) hybrid nanostructure was grown on Ni foam using ZIF-67 as precursor. The obtained catalyst demonstrates an excellent electrocatalytic performance for OER with an overpotential of \(275\ \mathrm{mV}\) at \(30\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\).[140] Tang group made further efforts on ZIF-67 derived hollow CoS. CoS was decorated with \(\mathrm{CeO}_{x}\) nanoparticles to form a novel hybrid nanostructure \(\mathrm{CeO}_{x}/\mathrm{CoS}\) (Figure 11) which reaches \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) at a low overpotential of \(269\ \mathrm{mV}\) in \(1\ \mathrm{M\ KOH}\).[141] A novel earthworm-like N, S-doped carbon nanotube-encapsulated \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) hybrid \(\left(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{CT}-800\right)\) was tailored as a highly efficient bifunctional oxygen catalyst by pyrolyzing the two-phase incorporation product Co(II) \(\mathrm{Thu}@\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Thu}=\mathrm{thiourea}\right)\) at \(800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The DFT theory shows that the excellent activity of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{CT}-800\) was ascribed to the synergistic effect of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and the N-doped graphitic shell.[142]
Nano-architecture engineering is another crucial method to generate more accessible active area for catalysts and facilitate mass transfer. Well-designed nanostructures, such as hollow structure and yolk-shell, exhibit promising electrocatalytic performance due to the more exposed accessible active sites than the bulk structure.[132] Hollow
nanostructure electrocatalysts for OER demonstrate a promising performance. Qiu et al. reported the fabrication of double-shelled hybrid nanocages with outer shells of Co-N-doped graphitic carbon (Co-NGC) and inner shells of Ni-doped microporous carbon derived from core-shell metal organic frameworks ZIF-8@ZIF-67 (Figure 12a–d).[132] Employing a novel MOF-hybrid-assisted strategy, Lou group synthesized \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Co}\) -Fe oxide double-shelled nanoboxes. Zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67)/Co-Fe PBA yolk-shell nanocubes are firstly formed by a facile anion-exchange reaction and subsequently subjected to an annealing treatment to obtain \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\) Co-Fe oxide double-shelled nanoboxes. And this metal oxide hybrid with a novel morphology exhibits an enhanced electrocatalytic performance for OER in alkaline solution.[143]
## 3.3. MOFs-Derived Electrocatalysts for ORR
In general, after pyrolysis MOFs-derived materials comprised carbon materials loaded metal nanoparticles, metal alloys, metal compounds, or metal single atom. However, sometimes MOFs would convert to other compounds without carbon in room temperature as needed (Table 6). MOFs-derived electrocatalyst \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}\) nanoparticles coated by a N, P-co-doped carbon shell \((\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{NPPC})\) were prepared using a Cu-based MOF in which the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) nodes were connected by two organic linkers, containing nitrogen and phosphorous heteroatoms as a single precursor and template. These novel \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}\) -based bifunctional catalysts demonstrate outstanding activity for ORR ascribed to the high specific surface area.[144]
Metal oxide or multiple metal oxide \(@\) carbon material derived from MOFs was investigated and presented expecting catalytic performance. Commercial carbon Vulcan XC72 combined with ZIF-67 was subjected to calcination process at \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain a low content of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) particles loaded on the N-doping carbon matrix, which achieved impressive ORR activity and stability.[145] A cobalt, nitrogen, and sulfur co-doped nanomaterial \(\mathrm{CoO@Co_{3}O_{4}/N S G-650}\) with a 3D hierarchical porous structure was fabricated utilizing ZIF67 and sulfur-functionalized graphene oxide as precursors. This
novel 3D crosslinked structure was composed of polyhedron particles throughout the whole carbon framework, which could speed up the mass transportation and charge-delivery process and devote to a good conductivity. The electrocatalytic measurements demonstrate that the \(\mathrm{CoO@Co_{3}O_{4}/N S G-650}\) showed comparable electrocatalytic performance and even better methanol tolerance than those of noble metal catalyst \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) for ORR.[146] Hollow \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanospheres embedded in nitrogen-doped carbon nanowall arrays on flexible carbon cloth \((\mathrm{NC-Co_{3}O_{4}/C C})\) with a hierarchical structure were facilely fabricated from a Co-based MOF through a combined carbonization–oxidation process. The prepared \(\mathrm{NC-Co_{3}O_{4}/C C}\) had achieved a promising catalytic performance toward both OER and ORR, even better, \(\mathrm{NC-Co_{3}O_{4}/C C}\) was first utilized as an integrated air cathode for flexible all-solid-state Zn-air batteries without any binder or additives.[147]
Many studies demonstrated that MOFs-derived carbon matrix loaded nanoparticles exhibited an efficient ORR performance. For instance, Li group reported a porous carbon nanotube loaded with cobalt nanoparticles derived from the pyrolysis of \(\mathrm{Co/Zn}\) bimetallic ZIF. The developed \(\mathrm{Co@NC}\) exhibits an outstanding bifunctional oxygen electrocatalytic performance ascribed to the synergistic effect of graphitic carbon, nanotubes, exposed \(\mathrm{Co-N_{x}}\) active sites, and interconnected framework structure of various carbons.[148] Dai et al. designed and synthesized a new class of Co @ N-Cs with a unique 3D hierarchical rod-like structure consisting of homogeneously distributed cobalt nanoparticles encapsulated by partially graphitized N-doped carbon rings along the rod length, derived from pyrolysis of a pair of enantiotopic chiral 3D MOFs. The newly developed bifunctional catalyst exhibits a higher electrocatalytic performance than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\).[149]
Furthermore, exploration on the nano-architecturing of MOF-derived carbon matrix facilitated the ORR performance.[150] Double-shelled hybrid nanocages NC @ Co-NGC, with outer shells of Co-N-doped graphitic carbon (Co-NGC) and inner shells of N-doped microporous carbon (NC), were synthesized by templating against core-shell MOFs. NC @ Co-NGC exhibited superior electrocatalytic properties than noble metal catalysts Pt for ORR and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) for OER.[132] Different from synthesizing bimetal ZIF with the
conventional shape of rhombic dodecahedron, a 2D dual-metal (Co/ Zn) leaf-like ZIF was first pyrolyzed for the nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (Co-N-CNTs) encapsulated Co nanoparticles.[151] A uniform virus-like Co–N–C nanomaterial with excellent bifunctional catalytic activity and cycling stability is achieved via a facile carbonization of a PBA.[152]
