yrolysis strategy. They employed ZIF-8 as molecular-scale cages to separate and encapsulate the metal precursor Fe(acac)3 (Fe(acac)3@ZIF-8). Subsequently, the obtained mixture underwent pyrolysis at 900 °C under Ar atmosphere to receive the desired catalyst Fe–SAs/CN (2.16 wt%). The catalyst represented an excellent ORR performance, a good methanol tolerance, and an outstanding stability.[159] Direct pyrolyzation of Fe-based MOFs is another efficient strategy to obtain Fe-containing single-atom catalysts. Hai-Long Jiang group prepared high-content (1.76 wt%) single-atom iron-implanted N-doped porous carbon (Fe5A–N–C) via pyrolysis of porphyrin MOFs. The optimized Fe5A–N–C exhibited more superior ORR activity and stability than Pt/C in both alkaline and acidic media due to the single-atom Fe sites, hierarchical pores, and high conductivity.[154] Furthermore, they developed a general synthetic methodology to obtain N-doped porous carbon supported high-content single-atom Fe (Fe5A–N–C) (3.46wt%) through pyrolyzing PCN-222(Fe) nanocasted SiO2 (SiO2@MOF composite) (Figure 13a). The high Fe loading profited from a dual protection from the spatial isolation of Fe atoms by porphyrin linkers in PCN-222 (Fe) and additional protection from thermally stable FeN4/SiO2 interfaces. Fe5A–N–C delivers excellent performance for ORR and acidic proton exchange membrane fuel cell (PEMFC) (Figure 13b–d).[160]Single stable Co atoms supporting on nitrogen-doped porous carbon (Co5A@NC) with a high metal loading exceeding 4 wt % were also reported as a superior ORR electrocatalyst. Co5A@NC is derived from a pyrolysis process of bimetallic Zn/Co MOFs and exhibited a superior ORR performance.[161] Subsequent research, which used Co5A@NC as the ORR catalyst in PEMFCs, verified that the respectable activity and stability for ORR was attributed to the formed CoN4 active sites.[72] To confirm the real active site of the metal/nitrogen-doped carbon with encapsulated metal nanoparticles' structure, 