To expose more accessible active sites, morphology engineering was conducted on MOFs-derived porous carbon, such as hollow nanocages, 1D nanotube, and a hierarchically ordered porous carbon structure.[163,164] Rationally designing highly effective electrocatalysts with \(\mathrm{Fe-N_{x}-C}\) active sites was also investigated continuously. Fe-, N-codoped porous carbon nanotubes with a novel hierarchical structure were designed by directly growing ZIF-8 on porous Tellurium nanotubes, introducing \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), and employing Tellurium as the sacrificial template. The synergistic effects between the porous carbon structure and active \(\mathrm{Fe-N_{x}-C}\) conferred this carbon framework superior catalytic activity toward ORR than Pt/C in both alkaline and acidic media.[165] An overhang-eave carbon structure decorated with single Fe atoms was designed via a silica-mediated MOF-templated approach (Figure 14), which exhibited an outstanding ORR performance in both alkaline and acidic media, superior to Pt/C.[155] More interestingly, in addition to the morphology engineering, the rationally designed preparation method could also expose the active sites to the maximum. Currently, Jia group adopted chemical vapor deposition by flowing Fe salt steam across the \(Z\mathrm{nN}_{4}\) substrate to form \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}}\) via metal replacement. Therefrom prepared Fe–N–C electrocatalyst delivered a \(100\%\) active site utilization.[166]
The strategies of the process of preparation of single-atom supported carbon materials mainly involve the removal of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) during pyrolysis and acid etching, while the removal of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) needs a high temperature
(single atom tends to aggregate) and the acid etching may destroy the carbon structure. Thus, Yang group used Cd as alternative to Zn and Fe salt coordinated with 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane and terephthalic acid to form MOF precursors. This MOF suffers from pyrolysis at \(750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\) atmosphere to obtain Fe–C–N750, which exhibits a significantly enhanced ORR performance than the contrast sample ZIF-8/Fe750.[167]
## 3.4. Identification of Active Sites During the Electrocatalytic Process
For MOFs-derived materials, the identification of active sites for HER is still in dispute. Some studies proved that metal sites played active roles during electrocatalytic HER.[171,172] And the electronic structure reconstruction of the metal resulting from another metals' introduction could improve the HER performance.[172] Meanwhile, other studies observed the formation of S-H bonding with the aid of surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) during the HER process, which proves the S atoms
that active \(\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}\) sites in electrocatalysts with \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) and Cu dual single sites evolved from the form of isolated sites to cluster during the electrocatalytic process, and reversibly changed into single atom again after removing the voltage.[180]
## 4. Conclusions and Perspectives
MOF-based materials, as a newly emerging class of porous materials, have achieved desirable performance in electrocatalysis. This review has been focused on recently developed original MOFs and their derived electrocatalysts. Currently, the overpotential of MOF-based OER electrocatalysts could drop down to within \(200\,\mathrm{\mV}\) at \(10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) on a GCE. As HER electrocatalysts, MOFs also achieve a minimum overpotential within \(30~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) . Surprisingly, MOFs-based ORR electrocatalysts could achieve a half-wave potential superior to \(1\ \mathrm{V},\) which undoubtedly paves the way for a renewable energy utilization, especially for fuel cells and metal-air batteries.
The rapid evolution of operando spectroscopy and microscopy techniques helps researchers to recog-ize and deepen their understanding of active sites and reaction mechanisms of electrocatalysts during the electrocatalytic process, which definitely makes for an efficient electrocatalyst design. For HER, both the nonmetallic atoms \((\mathrm{S/P/N})\) and metal sites are declared to be active sites during the electrocatalytic process. It is still controversial to point out which are the absolute active site. Most of the literature has demonstrated that in OER the metals play an active role during electrocatalysis, which gradually evolves into their corresponding hydroxides and oxyhydroxides at a higher potential. Despite the fact that M-N-C are deemed as active sites for ORR, the ocular proof related to their evolution mechanisms during electrolysis is rare, and at present it is explained mainly from the aspect of theory calculation.
Despite the emerging developments in the application of MOFsbased materials as electrocatalysts, there is some room for improvement or development direction in the future: 1) further modification on active sites to increase their utilization; 2) exploring another alternative reaction (5-hydroxymethylfurfural (HMF) electrooxidation, semi
dehydrogenation of tetrahydroisoquinolines, etc.) to OER with rapid kinetics and highvalue-added products; and 3) improving the long-term stability of MOFs-based catalysts in solution to guarantee their practical application in electrolyte solution.
